{"version":"6.9","hitCount":6,"request":{"queryString":"\"DNA origami\" AND \"crossover\" AND \"structural consequences\"","resultType":"core","cursorMark":"*","pageSize":25,"sort":"","synonym":false},"resultList":{"result":[{"id":"37159224","source":"MED","pmid":"37159224","pmcid":"PMC10210531","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC10210531"]},"doi":"10.1021/acsnano.2c11402","title":"Triple-Stranded DNA As a Structural Element in DNA Origami.","authorString":"Sachenbacher K, Khoshouei A, Honemann MN, Engelen W, Feigl E, Dietz H.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Sachenbacher K","firstName":"Ken","lastName":"Sachenbacher","initials":"K","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2230-4553"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biosciences, School of Natural Sciences and Munich Institute of Biomedical Engineering, Technical University Munich (TUM), Munich, Garching 85748, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Khoshouei A","firstName":"Ali","lastName":"Khoshouei","initials":"A","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1735-8423"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biosciences, School of Natural Sciences and Munich Institute of Biomedical Engineering, Technical University Munich (TUM), Munich, Garching 85748, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Honemann MN","firstName":"Maximilian Nicolas","lastName":"Honemann","initials":"MN","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biosciences, School of Natural Sciences and Munich Institute of Biomedical Engineering, Technical University Munich (TUM), Munich, Garching 85748, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Engelen W","firstName":"Wouter","lastName":"Engelen","initials":"W","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8779-9887"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biosciences, School of Natural Sciences and Munich Institute of Biomedical Engineering, Technical University Munich (TUM), Munich, Garching 85748, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Feigl E","firstName":"Elija","lastName":"Feigl","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biosciences, School of Natural Sciences and Munich Institute of Biomedical Engineering, Technical University Munich (TUM), Munich, Garching 85748, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Dietz H","firstName":"Hendrik","lastName":"Dietz","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1270-3662"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Biosciences, School of Natural Sciences and Munich Institute of Biomedical Engineering, Technical University Munich (TUM), Munich, Garching 85748, Germany."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-8779-9887"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0002-2230-4553"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1270-3662"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1735-8423"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"10","volume":"17","journalIssueId":3574525,"dateOfPublication":"2023 May","monthOfPublication":5,"yearOfPublication":2023,"printPublicationDate":"2023-05-01","journal":{"title":"ACS nano","medlineAbbreviation":"ACS Nano","isoabbreviation":"ACS Nano","essn":"1936-086X","issn":"1936-0851","nlmid":"101313589"}},"pubYear":"2023","pageInfo":"9014-9024","abstractText":"Molecular self-assembly with DNA origami offers an attractive route to fabricate arbitrary three-dimensional nanostructures. In DNA origami, B-form double-helical DNA domains (dsDNA) are commonly linked with covalent phosphodiester strand crossovers to build up three-dimensional objects. To expand the palette of structural motifs in DNA origami, here we describe hybrid duplex-triplex DNA motifs as pH-dependent building blocks in DNA origami. We investigate design rules for incorporating triplex forming oligonucleotides and noncanonical duplex-triplex crossovers in multilayer DNA origami objects. We use single-particle cryoelectron microscopy to elucidate the structural basis of triplex domains and of duplex-triplex crossovers. We find that duplex-triplex crossovers can complement and fully replace the canonical duplex-duplex crossovers within DNA origami objects, for example, to increase the crossover density for potentially greater rigidity and reduced interhelical spacing, and to create connections at sites where conventional crossovers may be undesirable. We also show the pH-induced formation of a DNA origami object stabilized entirely by triplex-mediated strand crossovers.","affiliation":"Department of Biosciences, School of Natural Sciences and Munich Institute of Biomedical Engineering, Technical University Munich (TUM), Munich, Garching 85748, Germany.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"899619","agency":"H2020 Future and Emerging Technologies","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"SFB863 TPA9","agency":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"019.182EN.037","agency":"Dutch Research Council (NWO)","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"101018465","agency":"European Research Council","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"DNA","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"CH","qualifierName":"chemistry","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Oligonucleotides","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"CH","qualifierName":"chemistry","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cryoelectron Microscopy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Nucleic Acid Conformation"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Nanostructures","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"CH","qualifierName":"chemistry","majorTopic_YN":"N"}]}}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Nanodevices","Dna Origami","Dna Triplex","Cryo Em","Ph Switching"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"DNA","registryNumber":"9007-49-2"},{"name":"Oligonucleotides","registryNumber":"0"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1021/acsnano.2c11402"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC10210531"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC10210531?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":17,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2023-05-24","dateOfCreation":"2023-05-09","firstIndexDate":"2023-05-09","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-02-02","dateOfRevision":"2023-05-27","electronicPublicationDate":"2023-05-09","firstPublicationDate":"2023-05-09"},{"id":"33724780","source":"MED","pmid":"33724780","pmcid":"PMC8223477","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC8223477"]},"doi":"10.1021/acsnano.0c10137","title":"Cryo-Electron Microscopy and Mass Analysis of Oligolysine-Coated DNA Nanostructures.","authorString":"Bertosin E, Stömmer P, Feigl E, Wenig M, Honemann MN, Dietz H.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Bertosin E","firstName":"Eva","lastName":"Bertosin","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Physics, Technical University of Munich, Garching 85748, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Stömmer P","firstName":"Pierre","lastName":"Stömmer","initials":"P","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1683-3392"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Physics, Technical University of Munich, Garching 85748, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Feigl E","firstName":"Elija","lastName":"Feigl","initials":"E","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Physics, Technical University of Munich, Garching 85748, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Wenig M","firstName":"Maximilian","lastName":"Wenig","initials":"M","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Physics, Technical University of Munich, Garching 85748, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Honemann MN","firstName":"Maximilian N","lastName":"Honemann","initials":"MN","authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Physics, Technical University of Munich, Garching 85748, Germany."}]}},{"fullName":"Dietz H","firstName":"Hendrik","lastName":"Dietz","initials":"H","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1270-3662"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Physics, Technical University of Munich, Garching 85748, Germany."}]}}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1270-3662"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0003-1683-3392"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","supporting_data"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"6","volume":"15","journalIssueId":3190802,"dateOfPublication":"2021 Jun","monthOfPublication":6,"yearOfPublication":2021,"printPublicationDate":"2021-06-01","journal":{"title":"ACS nano","medlineAbbreviation":"ACS Nano","isoabbreviation":"ACS Nano","essn":"1936-086X","issn":"1936-0851","nlmid":"101313589"}},"pubYear":"2021","pageInfo":"9391-9403","abstractText":"Cationic coatings can enhance the stability of synthetic DNA objects in low ionic strength environments such as physiological fluids. Here, we used single-particle cryo-electron microscopy (cryo-EM), pseudoatomic model fitting, and single-molecule mass photometry to study oligolysine and polyethylene glycol (PEG)-oligolysine-coated multilayer DNA origami objects. The coatings preserve coarse structural features well on a resolution of multiple nanometers but can also induce deformations such as twisting and bending. Higher-density coatings also led to internal structural deformations in the DNA origami test objects, in which a designed honeycomb-type helical lattice was deformed into a more square-lattice-like pattern. Under physiological ionic strength, where the uncoated objects disassembled, the coated objects remained intact but they shrunk in the helical direction and expanded in the direction perpendicular to the helical axis. Helical details like major/minor grooves and crossover locations were not discernible in cryo-EM maps that we determined of DNA origami coated with oligolysine and PEG-oligolysine, whereas these features were visible in cryo-EM maps determined from the uncoated reference objects. Blunt-ended double-helical interfaces remained accessible underneath the coating and may be used for the formation of multimeric DNA origami assemblies that rely on stacking interactions between blunt-ended helices. The ionic strength requirements for forming multimers from coated DNA origami differed from those needed for uncoated objects. Using single-molecule mass photometry, we found that the mass of coated DNA origami objects prior to and after incubation in low ionic strength physiological conditions remained unchanged. This finding indicated that the coating effectively prevented strand dissociation but also that the coating itself remained stable in place. Our results validate oligolysine coatings as a powerful stabilization method for DNA origami but also reveal several potential points of failure that experimenters should watch to avoid working with false premises.","affiliation":"Department of Physics, Technical University of Munich, Garching 85748, Germany.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"724261","agency":"European Research Council","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Bundesministerium f?r Bildung und Forschung","orderIn":0},{"grantId":"SFB863 TPA9/ID 111166240","agency":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","orderIn":0},{"agency":"Max-Planck-Gesellschaft","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"DNA"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Cryoelectron Microscopy"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Nucleic Acid Conformation"},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"Nanotechnology"},{"majorTopic_YN":"Y","descriptorName":"Nanostructures"}]},"keywordList":{"keyword":["Self-assembly","Dna Nanotechnology","Dna Origami","Cryo-em","Mass Photometry","Peg-oligolysine","Pseudoatomic Model Fitting"]},"chemicalList":{"chemical":[{"name":"DNA","registryNumber":"9007-49-2"}]},"subsetList":{"subset":[{"code":"IM","name":"Index Medicus"}]},"fullTextUrlList":{"fullTextUrl":[{"availability":"Subscription required","availabilityCode":"S","documentStyle":"doi","site":"DOI","url":"https://doi.org/10.1021/acsnano.0c10137"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"html","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC8223477"},{"availability":"Open access","availabilityCode":"OA","documentStyle":"pdf","site":"Europe_PMC","url":"https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC8223477?pdf=render"}]},"isOpenAccess":"Y","inEPMC":"Y","inPMC":"Y","hasPDF":"Y","hasBook":"N","hasSuppl":"Y","citedByCount":31,"hasData":"Y","hasReferences":"Y","hasTextMinedTerms":"Y","hasDbCrossReferences":"N","hasLabsLinks":"Y","license":"cc by-nc-nd","hasEvaluations":"N","authMan":"N","epmcAuthMan":"N","nihAuthMan":"N","hasTMAccessionNumbers":"N","dateOfCompletion":"2021-07-14","dateOfCreation":"2021-03-16","firstIndexDate":"2021-03-17","fullTextReceivedDate":"2025-02-04","dateOfRevision":"2021-07-14","electronicPublicationDate":"2021-03-16","firstPublicationDate":"2021-03-16"},{"id":"19531737","source":"MED","pmid":"19531737","pmcid":"PMC2731887","fullTextIdList":{"fullTextId":["PMC2731887"]},"doi":"10.1093/nar/gkp436","title":"Rapid prototyping of 3D DNA-origami shapes with caDNAno.","authorString":"Douglas SM, Marblestone AH, Teerapittayanon S, Vazquez A, Church GM, Shih WM.","authorList":{"author":[{"fullName":"Douglas SM","firstName":"Shawn M","lastName":"Douglas","initials":"SM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5398-9041"},"authorAffiliationDetailsList":{"authorAffiliation":[{"affiliation":"Department of Cancer Biology, Dana-Farber Cancer Institute, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA 02115, USA."}]}},{"fullName":"Marblestone AH","firstName":"Adam H","lastName":"Marblestone","initials":"AH","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9833-9931"}},{"fullName":"Teerapittayanon S","firstName":"Surat","lastName":"Teerapittayanon","initials":"S"},{"fullName":"Vazquez A","firstName":"Alejandro","lastName":"Vazquez","initials":"A"},{"fullName":"Church GM","firstName":"George M","lastName":"Church","initials":"GM","authorId":{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6232-9969"}},{"fullName":"Shih WM","firstName":"William M","lastName":"Shih","initials":"WM"}]},"authorIdList":{"authorId":[{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-5398-9041"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-6232-9969"},{"type":"ORCID","value":"0000-0001-9833-9931"}]},"dataLinksTagsList":{"dataLinkstag":["altmetrics","wikipedia"]},"journalInfo":{"issue":"15","volume":"37","journalIssueId":1646254,"dateOfPublication":"2009 Aug","monthOfPublication":8,"yearOfPublication":2009,"printPublicationDate":"2009-08-01","journal":{"title":"Nucleic acids research","medlineAbbreviation":"Nucleic Acids Res","isoabbreviation":"Nucleic Acids Res","essn":"1362-4962","issn":"0305-1048","nlmid":"0411011"}},"pubYear":"2009","pageInfo":"5001-5006","abstractText":"DNA nanotechnology exploits the programmable specificity afforded by base-pairing to produce self-assembling macromolecular objects of custom shape. For building megadalton-scale DNA nanostructures, a long 'scaffold' strand can be employed to template the assembly of hundreds of oligonucleotide 'staple' strands into a planar antiparallel array of cross-linked helices. We recently adapted this 'scaffolded DNA origami' method to producing 3D shapes formed as pleated layers of double helices constrained to a honeycomb lattice. However, completing the required design steps can be cumbersome and time-consuming. Here we present caDNAno, an open-source software package with a graphical user interface that aids in the design of DNA sequences for folding 3D honeycomb-pleated shapes A series of rectangular-block motifs were designed, assembled, and analyzed to identify a well-behaved motif that could serve as a building block for future studies. The use of caDNAno significantly reduces the effort required to design 3D DNA-origami structures. The software is available at http://cadnano.org/, along with example designs and video tutorials demonstrating their construction. The source code is released under the MIT license.","affiliation":"Department of Cancer Biology, Dana-Farber Cancer Institute, Harvard Medical School, Boston, MA 02115, USA.","publicationStatus":"ppublish","language":"eng","pubModel":"Print-Electronic","pubTypeList":{"pubType":["Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't","research-article","Journal Article","Research Support, N.I.H., Extramural"]},"grantsList":{"grant":[{"grantId":"1DP2OD004641-01","agency":"NIH HHS","acronym":"OD","orderIn":0}]},"meshHeadingList":{"meshHeading":[{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorName":"DNA","meshQualifierList":{"meshQualifier":[{"abbreviation":"UL","qualifierName":"ultrastructure","majorTopic_YN":"N"},{"abbreviation":"CH","qualifierName":"chemistry","majorTopic_YN":"Y"}]}},{"majorTopic_YN":"N","descriptorN